ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

HOST-SIDE SQLITE EXPERIMENT CATALOG

The dashboard includes a host-side SQLite catalog for recording benchmark
experiment metadata. It is separate from the xv6 kernel and does not change
kernel telemetry behavior. From the project directory, run:

  python3 dashboard.py

Then open http://localhost:8000. The SQLite catalog supports creating,
searching, editing, and deleting experiment records in the
"dbms_experiments" table in xv6_analytics.db. Its schema is in
dbms_schema.sql.

SIMULATED SCHEDULING AND PAGE REPLACEMENT

The dashboard also includes a separate simulator. Scheduling runs accept
arrival time, burst time, and priority for each process, and support FCFS,
SJF, SRTF, Priority, and Round Robin (with a time quantum). SJF and Priority
are non-preemptive; SRTF and Round Robin are preemptive. Lower numeric
priorities run first.

Paging runs accept a comma- or space-separated page reference string and a
frame count, then simulate FIFO or LRU. Results include per-process scheduling
times or each page reference's hit/fault and frame contents. Runs are stored
separately in the "dbms_simulation_runs", "dbms_schedule_results", and
"dbms_paging_steps" tables. These are simulated results, not xv6 measurements.
